{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "stockmark/stockmark-2-100b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III, Count of Tripoli, also known as Raymond de Saint-Gilles, was a prominent figure in the Crusader states of the Levant during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. He was born in 1135, the son of Bertrand of Saint-Gilles and Hodierna of Tripoli, and inherited the county of Tripoli in 1152 following the death of his father.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*:\nRaymond III's reign was marked by significant military and political challenges. He played a crucial role in the Crusades, particularly the Third Crusade. In 1187, he was taken prisoner by Saladin and released after the Siege of Acre in 1191. His participation in the Crusades was not limited to the Third Crusade; he also led several other military campaigns to defend the Crusader states from various threats.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*:\nDuring his rule, Raymond III faced numerous conflicts with neighboring powers. He was known for his strategic alliances and his efforts to expand Tripoli's influence. One of the most notable was his support for the Templars and the Hospitallers, two military orders that played pivotal roles in the Crusades. His reign saw significant construction and development in Tripoli, including the fortification of the city and the establishment of various religious institutions.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*:\nRaymond III's legacy is complex. While he was a fierce defender of the Crusader states and a capable military leader, his personal life was marred by scandal. He was involved in a controversial marriage to the widow of his cousin, which led to accusations of incest. Despite these controversies, he is remembered for his contributions to the Crusades and his efforts to strengthen the Crusader states in the Levant. His reign ended with his death in 1187, but his impact on the region and the Crusader history persisted.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ra